@page "/help"

@using TCalcCore.Engine
@inject TourcalcEngine engine
@inject UiModeService uiMode
@implements IDisposable

@* Written against what the app actually does, not against what it was meant to do:
   the counting rules below are the ones in TourPageNew / ExpensesTab, and the wording
   matches the labels on screen so that a term can be looked up as it is seen. *@

@{
    engine.SendMessage("Title", "Help");
    engine.SendMessage("Tour", null);
}

<article class="tcn-help">

    <header class="tcn-help-hero">
        <div class="tcn-help-logo">🧭</div>
        <h1 class="tcn-help-title">Tourcalc</h1>
        <p class="tcn-help-lead">
            A shared wallet for a trip. Everyone pays for whatever is convenient at the moment —
            one buys the tickets, another the groceries, a third the taxi — and nobody counts
            anything until the end. Tourcalc keeps the record and, when you are done, says who
            has to pay whom and how much, in as few payments as possible.
        </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="tcn-help-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="help#start">Getting started</a>
        <a href="help#people">People and shares</a>
        <a href="help#expenses">Recording an expense</a>
        <a href="help#counted">What counts as spending</a>
        <a href="help#balance">Balances and settling up</a>
        <a href="help#families">Paying for someone else</a>
        <a href="help#currencies">Several currencies</a>
        <a href="help#sharing">Sharing a tour</a>
        <a href="help#offline">Offline and refreshing</a>
        <a href="help#glossary">Every word on screen</a>
        <a href="help#trouble">When the numbers look wrong</a>
    </nav>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="start">
        <h2>Getting started</h2>
        <ol class="tcn-help-steps">
            <li><b>Create a tour.</b> One tour is one trip, one flat, one project — any group of people with a common pot. <b>+ New tour</b> on the tour list.</li>
            <li><b>Add the people.</b> <b>People → + Add person</b>. Names only; no accounts, no invitations.</li>
            <li><b>Record expenses as they happen.</b> The <b>+ Spend</b> button is on every screen. Amount, what it was for, who paid, who it was for.</li>
            <li><b>At the end, open <b>Balance</b>.</b> It shows the payments that square everyone up. When a payment actually happens, press <b>Mark paid</b>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="tcn-help-note">
            Nothing here is money. Tourcalc moves no funds and talks to no bank — it is a
            notebook that can do arithmetic.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="people">
        <h2>People and shares</h2>
        <p>
            A shared expense is split by <b>weight</b>, not by headcount. A full share is
            <b>100</b>. Someone on <b>50</b> pays for half as much of everything shared —
            a child, or a person who joined for half the trip. A custom <b>0</b> means they
            pay for nothing at all while still appearing in the list.
        </p>
        <p>
            Weights only matter relative to each other: three people on 100 and three on 200
            split exactly like 50 / 100. The People tab shows the total weight, and tapping it
            explains how much of a shared expense one full share carries.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="expenses">
        <h2>Recording an expense</h2>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b>Amount</b></div>
            <div>What was actually paid. In a multi-currency tour, pick the currency it was paid in.</div>
            <div><b>What for</b></div>
            <div>The description you will recognise later in the list.</div>
            <div><b>Paid by</b></div>
            <div>The person whose money left the pocket. Pre-filled with whoever you last recorded as paying.</div>
            <div><b>Split between</b></div>
            <div><b>everyone</b> shares it by weight. Turn it off to pick particular people — it is then split between just those, still by weight. (Older expenses may carry a <span class="tcn-chip">split equally</span> mark: those ignore weights and divide by headcount.)</div>
            <div><b>Category</b></div>
            <div>Groups the statistics. A new expense arrives with the last used category already picked; while it is only a guess it is drawn with a dashed outline and the note <i>“Last used”</i>. Tap any category to make it a decision.</div>
            <div><b>Date</b></div>
            <div>Defaults to today. Change it when you are catching up on yesterday.</div>
        </div>
        <h3>More options</h3>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b>Draft</b></div>
            <div>A possible expense, not a real one. It stays in the list marked <span class="tcn-chip tcn-chip-amber">draft&nbsp;·&nbsp;not counted</span> and changes no balance — useful for “what if we rent the boat”. Ticking <b>Still count it in the balance</b> makes it count while keeping the label.</div>
            <div><b>Colour</b></div>
            <div>Marks a rare, out-of-the-ordinary expense — a tourist tax for the whole group, an unexpected fine — so it catches the eye when you scroll the list. It has no effect on any calculation.</div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="counted">
        <h2>What counts as spending</h2>
        <p>
            <b>Total spent</b>, the day totals and the summary above the list all count the same
            thing: an expense that has a <b>category</b> and is <b>not a draft</b> (or is a draft
            you told it to count).
        </p>
        <p>Everything else is money that moved without being spent, and it is shown separately as <b>uncounted</b>:</p>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b>settling up</b></div>
            <div>Someone handing money to someone else inside the group. It is a transfer, not a purchase — counting it would count the same dinner twice. An expense with <b>no category</b> is treated as exactly this.</div>
            <div><b>draft</b></div>
            <div>Not real yet, so not spent yet.</div>
        </div>
        <p>
            Tapping the <b>uncounted</b> figure lists what is in it and why. This is the single
            most common source of “the total looks wrong” — it is usually right, and it is
            usually a payback.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="balance">
        <h2>Balances and settling up</h2>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b>Paid</b></div>
            <div>What this person put in — everything they paid for, for anyone.</div>
            <div><b>Charged</b></div>
            <div>What their own part of the spending cost — their share of everything that was shared with them.</div>
            <div><b>Balance</b></div>
            <div>Charged minus Paid. <b>owes</b> means their share cost more than they put in; <b>gets</b> means the group owes them.</div>
        </div>
        <p>
            All three open an itemised list when tapped, whatever the settings say.
        </p>
        <h3>Who pays whom</h3>
        <p>
            The Balance tab does not simply list debts — it works out the shortest set of
            payments that brings everyone to zero, so five people rarely need more than four
            transfers. <b>Nothing on that list has happened yet.</b> When the money really
            changes hands, press <b>Mark paid</b>: it records a payback (it appears in the list
            as <span class="tcn-chip">payback</span>) and both balances move towards zero.
        </p>
        <p class="tcn-help-note">
            Small leftovers are ignored on purpose. <b>Settings → Ignore debts smaller than</b>
            (49 by default) decides what counts as “already square”, so nobody is asked to
            transfer three coins.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="families">
        <h2>Paying for someone else</h2>
        <p>
            A child, a partner, a friend who is out of cash — someone whose share you settle
            yourself. In the person’s card, set <b>Paid for by</b> to whoever covers them.
            They keep their own weight and their own share of every expense, but they hand
            nothing over: their debt is folded into their payer’s.
        </p>
        <p>
            That is why a covered person shows a grey <span class="tcn-chip">owes …</span>
            rather than a red one, and why their payer can show two numbers: the settlement
            total, and <b>own</b> — their personal part of it, before the people they cover.
            Transfers inside a family are listed separately, under <b>Inside families</b>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="currencies">
        <h2>Several currencies</h2>
        <p>
            Two different things are easy to confuse, so they live in two different places:
        </p>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b><TcIcon Name="settings" /> currencies</b></div>
            <div>The <b>main currency</b> of the tour and the rates. It is a property of the tour: changing it changes what everyone sees.</div>
            <div><b>show amounts in</b></div>
            <div>A local view switch. It converts what is on your screen and saves nothing.</div>
        </div>
        <p>
            Rates are given as <b>worth</b>, on any scale you like — only the ratio matters. If
            one euro is 118 dinars, put 100 next to the dinar and 11800 next to the euro.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every expense stays attached to the currency it was entered in. <b>Renaming a
            currency is safe</b> — the amounts keep their meaning. <b>Removing one is not</b>:
            the expenses entered in it are then read in the main currency, and the numbers
            change.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="sharing">
        <h2>Sharing a tour</h2>
        <p>
            <b>share link</b> in the tour header copies an invite link to the clipboard.
            Opening that link logs the other person in by itself — no code to type, no account
            to create.
        </p>
        <div class="tcn-help-note">
            The link carries the <b>access code</b> of the tour, and that code opens
            <b>every tour created under it</b> — not only the one you meant to share. Whoever
            gets the link can also edit everything they can see. Treat it like a key, and use a
            separate code for anything you do not want in the same bundle.
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">
            Typing that same code on the sign-in screen is the other way in.
        </p>
        <p>
            Turning on <b>Push notifications</b> in the settings adds a bell to each tour, so
            the browser can tell you when somebody else changes it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="offline">
        <h2>Offline and refreshing</h2>
        <p>
            The tour is kept on the device, so it opens and edits without a connection; changes
            are sent when there is one. The line under the tour name says which copy you are
            looking at — <b>from server</b> or <b>local copy</b> — and how old it is.
        </p>
        <p>The <TcIcon Name="refresh" /> button asks the server and then says what happened, plainly:</p>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b>new data received</b></div>
            <div>Somebody had changed something; you are looking at it now.</div>
            <div><b>server has nothing newer</b></div>
            <div>The request went through and came back with the same tour.</div>
            <div><b>server did not answer</b></div>
            <div>No connection. What you see is the local copy, and it is still safe to edit.</div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="glossary">
        <h2>Every word on screen</h2>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b>Tour</b></div>
            <div>One group with one common pot.</div>
            <div><b>Weight / share</b></div>
            <div>How large a part of a shared expense a person carries. 100 is a full share.</div>
            <div><b>Paid / Charged / Balance</b></div>
            <div>Put in / own part cost / the difference.</div>
            <div><b>owes … / gets …</b></div>
            <div>Which way the difference points.</div>
            <div><b>Left to settle</b></div>
            <div>The sum of the payments still to be made between people.</div>
            <div><b>Mark paid</b></div>
            <div>Records that a suggested payment really happened.</div>
            <div><span class="tcn-chip">payback</span></div>
            <div>The record “A gave B this much”. Money moved, nothing was bought.</div>
            <div><span class="tcn-chip">inside family</span></div>
            <div>The same, between a person and whoever pays for them.</div>
            <div><span class="tcn-chip tcn-chip-amber">draft</span></div>
            <div>A possible expense. Counts towards nothing unless you say so.</div>
            <div><b>uncounted</b></div>
            <div>Money that moved without being spent: paybacks and drafts.</div>
            <div><span class="tcn-chip tcn-chip-amber">settling up</span></div>
            <div>On a tour: the trip is over and you are squaring up. It opens on the Balance tab.</div>
            <div><span class="tcn-chip">archived</span></div>
            <div>Hidden from the tour list until you tick <b>Show archived</b>.</div>
            <div><b>Main currency</b></div>
            <div>The one every total is calculated in. Shared by everyone.</div>
            <div><b>show amounts in</b></div>
            <div>Converts your own screen. Saved nowhere.</div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tcn-help-sec" id="trouble">
        <h2>When the numbers look wrong</h2>
        <div class="tcn-help-terms">
            <div><b>“Total spent is less than what we actually spent”</b></div>
            <div>Paybacks and drafts are not spending. Tap the <b>uncounted</b> figure next to the total to see exactly what was left out.</div>
            <div><b>“This expense is not in the statistics”</b></div>
            <div>It has no category. An expense without one is treated as settling up.</div>
            <div><b>“Nobody owes anything, but we clearly do”</b></div>
            <div>Debts under <b>Ignore debts smaller than</b> are treated as settled. Lower it in the settings if you really do split coins.</div>
            <div><b>“One person shows two different debts”</b></div>
            <div>They pay for someone. The large figure is what they hand over in total, <b>own</b> is their personal part.</div>
            <div><b>“I do not recognise these amounts”</b></div>
            <div>Check <b>show amounts in</b> in the tour header — you may be looking at another currency.</div>
            <div><b>“Somebody changed something and I cannot see it”</b></div>
            <div>Press <TcIcon Name="refresh" /> next to the tour name. If it says the server did not answer, you are offline and looking at the local copy.</div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="tcn-help-foot">
        <a class="tcn-btn" href="/settings"><TcIcon Name="settings" /> Settings</a>
        <a class="tcn-btn tcn-btn-primary" href="/tourlist">Back to your tours</a>
    </footer>

</article>

<AnchorNavigation />

@code {
    protected override void OnInitialized() => uiMode.OnChanged += OnUiModeChanged;

    public void Dispose() => uiMode.OnChanged -= OnUiModeChanged;

    private void OnUiModeChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
}
